.TH "FM" "1" "" "" "fm"
.SH NAME
fm \- Markdown frontmatter batch editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]fm\f[R] [\f[I]QUERY\f[R]] [\f[I]FLAGS\f[R]]
.PP
\f[B]fm\f[R] \f[B]completion\f[R] {bash|zsh|fish}
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]fm\f[R] queries and mutates YAML frontmatter in Markdown files
using an SQL\-style DSL.
It is aimed at knowledge\-management workflows such as Obsidian vaults,
where structured metadata sits in the \f[CR]\-\-\-\f[R] YAML block at
the top of \f[CR].md\f[R] files.
The same block may also sit in a comment in a source file
(\f[CR]# \-\-\-\f[R] in Python or shell, \f[CR]// \-\-\-\f[R] or
\f[CR]/*\-\-\-\f[R] in JavaScript, \&...); the comment syntax is chosen
per file by extension or shebang, or fixed with \f[B]\-\-lang\f[R],
\f[B]\-\-line\-comment\f[R] or \f[B]\-\-block\-open\f[R].
.PP
Three top\-level statements are supported:
.IP
.EX
select <exprs> from <globs> [where <expr>] [sort by <terms>] [limit <n>]
update <globs> set <assigns> [where <expr>]
alter  <globs> drop   <fields>  [where <expr>]
alter  <globs> rename <pairs>   [where <expr>]
.EE
.PP
Multiple statements may be combined into an SQL\-style script separated
by \f[CR];\f[R], with \f[CR]\-\-\f[R] as a line comment.
Scripts can be read from stdin or passed as a single positional
argument.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R], \f[B]\(enhelp\f[R]
Show usage and exit.
.TP
\f[B]\-d\f[R], \f[B]\(endry\-run\f[R]
Simulate the operation without editing any files.
Rejected for multi\-statement scripts.
.TP
\f[B]\-H\f[R], \f[B]\(enhidden\f[R]
Include hidden files (ignored by default).
.TP
\f[B]\-\-max\-columns\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Column cap for \f[B]select *\f[R] output.
Default 20.
.TP
\f[B]\-L\f[R], \f[B]\-\-lang\f[R] \f[I]LANG\f[R]
Parse every file as language \f[I]LANG\f[R], given as a name
(\f[CR]python\f[R], \f[CR]shell\f[R], \f[CR]c\f[R], \f[CR]sql\f[R],
\&...)
or an extension (\f[CR]py\f[R], \f[CR]js\f[R], \&...).
By default the syntax is chosen per file by extension or shebang.
With this flag a glob such as \f[B]*\f[R] matches files of every type.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-line\-comment\f[R] \f[I]STR\f[R]
Frontmatter is in line comments starting with \f[I]STR\f[R], for every
file.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-block\-open\f[R] \f[I]STR\f[R], \f[B]\-\-block\-close\f[R] \f[I]STR\f[R]
Frontmatter is in a block comment delimited by these markers, for every
file.
The closer defaults to the opener.
May be combined with \f[B]\-\-line\-comment\f[R] but not with
\f[B]\-\-lang\f[R].
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
\f[B]fm completion\f[R] \f[I]SHELL\f[R]
Print a shell\-completion script for \f[I]SHELL\f[R] (one of
\f[CR]bash\f[R], \f[CR]zsh\f[R], \f[CR]fish\f[R]) to stdout.
.SH EXAMPLES
Inspect frontmatter across a vault:
.IP
.EX
fm \(aqselect title, tags from notes/**/*.md\(aq
.EE
.PP
Cast a field to int across many files:
.IP
.EX
fm \(aqupdate recipes/* set \(gaPrep time\(ga:int where \(gaPrep time\(ga\(aq
.EE
.PP
Drop deprecated fields:
.IP
.EX
fm \(aqalter notes/* drop legacy_field\(aq
.EE
.SH FILES
\f[B]fm\f[R] reads and writes the file(s) matched by the \f[B]from\f[R]
clause in place.
There is no transactional layer; consider running on files under version
control or backed up.
